  "lineage": "Martinican rural carousel-music (chouval bwa is Martinican Creole for wooden horse, referring to the merry-go-round and travelling fair context where the music traditionally accompanied the carousel ride) — built on accordion (the melodic instrument), tambour bélé (the large Martinican single-headed barrel drum carried on the shoulder), ti-bwa (small bamboo struck-percussion), and chacha (Martinican calabash rattle); codified through 20th-century figures Eugène Mona (the Martinican flute virtuoso who incorporated chouval-bwa elements into his fusion work), Dédé Saint-Prix (the chouval-bwa accordionist), Tipany — distinct from the kindred Martinican biguine and zouk traditions",
